reasoning:
The minimal test reproduces! `std::string p = std::string(argv[0]) + "/magic"` — the bytes of p are poisoned because **libstdc++ is uninstrumented**! The string copy operations (std::string assignment/concatenation) are in uninstrumented libstdc++ → shadow of heap memory remains poisoned. That's the classic "MSan requires instrumented C++ stdlib" problem. The magic_fuzzer.cc uses `std::string data_dir`, `std::string magic_path = data_dir + "/magic"` — with uninstrumented libstdc++, magic_path bytes are poisoned.

So OSS-Fuzz must handle this... Actually NO — in OSS-Fuzz, C++ fuzz targets with MSan DO use the instrumented libcxx (they build with -stdlib=libc++ and a custom MSan-instrumented libc++). Right — OSS-Fuzz MSan builds install an MSan-instrumented libc++ at /usr/local/lib/cxx, and pass -stdlib=libc++.

So locally I should install an instrumented libc++... or simpler: avoid libstdc++ std::string in MY driver (use char* only). I only need local verification; the judge's harness is theirs. Let me rewrite my driver without std::string:

```cpp
int main(int argc, char** argv) {
  magic_t magic = magic_open(MAGIC_NONE);
  // construct path /tmp/out/magic with C strings
  ...
}
```

But careful: the real fuzzer's magic_open etc. — driver details don't matter for verification; I just need magic_buffer with the real magic.mgc and MSan, then feed input. Let me rewrite the driver in C-ish style with explicit char buffers and __msan_unpoison where needed.

Actually even simpler: keep it all C, no libstdc++. Then, with uninstrumented glibc: will magic_load + magic_buffer still hit glibc-related false positives? glibc functions like read() are intercepted by MSan (they unpoison). regcomp/regexec are NOT intercepted — that's exactly our bug. strchr/strdup etc. are intercepted. So a pure-C driver should load magic fine.

